a	2026-02-12-22-Building-a-Modular-Application-with-apywire-and-starlette2026-02-12-22-Construindo-uma-Aplicacao-Modular-com-apywire-e-starlette.pt
t	Building a Modular Application with apywire and starlette
i	Alexandre Gomes Gaigalas – February 12, 2026
p	xThis blog post explains how to use apywire to build a modular, dependency-injected starlette application. We’ll go step-by-step to wire up services, handlers, and a database using a declarative mconfig.yamlx.
p	xThe complete code for this tutorial is available at kgithub.com/alganet/apywire-starletted (https://github.com/alganet/apywire-starlette)x.
h	The Problem
p	xIn many Python web applications, wiring dependencies (like database connections, services, and controllers) is often done manually in the application entrypoint or using frameworks that rely heavily on runtime reflection and decorators. This can lead to:
b	xTightly coupled code: Hard to test and refactor.
b	xRuntime overhead: Reflection slows down startup and request handling.
b	xImplicit dependencies: Hard to see the full dependency graph.
p	xapywire solves this by providing a declarative way to define your dependency graph in a YAML file, which is then compiled into efficient, transparent Python code.
h	Step 1: The Components
p	xFirst, let’s define our application components as plain Python classes. Notice they have no dependency on mapywirex or any DI framework. They just declare what they need in m__init__x.
h	Services (src/services.py)
c	Kclass. LUserService.:
c	.    Kdef. F__init__.(.self., .db.):
c	.        .self...db. = .db
c	
c	.    Kdef. Fget_user.(.self., .screen_name.: Lstr.):
c	.        C# ... logic to fetch user ...
c	.        Kpass
c	
c	Kclass. LMigrationService.:
c	.    Kdef. F__init__.(.self., .db.):
c	.        .self...db. = .db
c	
c	.    Kdef. Frun.(.self.):
c	.        C# ... logic to run migrations ...
c	.        Kpass
c	
h	Handlers (src/handlers.py)
p	xOur handlers (controllers) are simple callables that take dependencies.
c	Kfrom. Lstarlette.responses. Kimport. LJSONResponse
c	Kfrom. Lstarlette.requests. Kimport. LRequest
c	Kimport. Lservices
c	
c	Kclass. LUserHandler.:
c	.    Kdef. F__init__.(.self., .users.: Lservices.UserService.):
c	.        .self...users. = .users
c	
c	.    Kasync. Kdef. F__call__.(.self., .scope., .receive., .send.):
c	.        .request. = LRequest.(.scope., .receive.)
c	.        .screen_name. = .request..path_params[S"screen_name".]
c	.        .user. = .self...users..Fget_user.(.screen_name.)
c	.        C# ... return response ...
c	
h	Step 2: The Wiring (config.yaml)
p	xThis is where the magic happens. We declare our dependency graph in mconfig.yamlx.
c	C# 1. Define the Database Connection - We use apsw directly, no wrapper needed.
c	Lapsw.Connection. .dbO:
c	.  AfilenameO:. S"db.sqlite"
c	
c	C# 2. Wire the Services - We inject the 'db' defined above into our services.
c	Lservices.UserService. .usersO:
c	.  .dbO:. O{.dbO}
c	
c	Lservices.MigrationService. .migrationsO:
c	.  .dbO:. O{.dbO}
c	
c	C# 3. Wire the Handlers - We inject the 'users' service into the UserHandler.
c	Lhandlers.UserHandler. .user_handlerO:
c	.  .usersO:. O{.usersO}
c	
c	Lhandlers.HomeHandler. .home_handlerO:. O{O}
c	
c	C# 4. Define Routes - We bind the 'user_handler' to a path.
c	Lstarlette.routing.Route. .user_routeO:
c	.  ApathO:. S"/users/O{.screen_nameO}S"
c	.  AendpointO:. O{.user_handlerO}
c	.  AmethodsO:. O[S"GET"O]
c	
c	Lstarlette.routing.Route. .hello_routeO:
c	.  ApathO:. S"/"
c	.  AendpointO:. O{.home_handlerO}
c	
c	C# 5. The Application - We assemble the app with our routes.
c	Lstarlette.applications.starlette. .appO:
c	.  AroutesO:
c	.    O-. O{.user_routeO}
c	.    O-. O{.hello_routeO}
c	
p	xKey takeaways:
b	m{db}x: References the component named mdbx.
b	xFully Qualified Names: We use mmodule.ClassNamex to specify types.
b	xNo Code Changes: We didn't touch our Python classes to wire them up.
h	Step 3: Compilation
p	xapywire compiles this YAML into a Python file (msrc/container.pyx). This generated code is what your application actually uses. It handles lazy loading and ensures singletons are shared correctly.
p	xTo compile:
c	.python src/app.py --compile
p	xThis generates msrc/container.pyx. You don't edit this file, but you can inspect it to see exactly how your application is wired. It’s just plain Python code!
h	Step 4: Running the App
p	xOur entrypoint msrc/app.pyx simply imports the compiled container and uses it.
c	Kfrom. Lcontainer. Kimport. Lcompiled
c	
c	Kdef. Fcreate_app.():
c	.    Kreturn. Lcompiled..app()
c	
c	Kif. .__name__. == S"__main__".:
c	.    C# ... CLI logic ...
c	.    Luvicorn..Frun.(S"app:create_app"., factory=KTrue.)
c	
p	xBecause mcompiledx is imported from a generated file, there is zero framework overhead at runtime.
h	Conclusion
p	xBy isolating wiring into mconfig.yamlx, we achieved:
b	xClean Architecture: Business logic knows nothing about the framework.
b	xPerformance: Compilation effectively "bakes" the dependency graph into optimized code.
b	xFlexibility: Changing the database implementation or swapping a service is just a config change.
p	xGive kapywired (https://github.com/alganet/apywire)x a try for your next Python project!
